<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Central America</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Central America is the narrow land bridge between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, today divided among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, Belize, Honduras, El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>, Nicaragua, Costa Rica and Panama. Its Pacific lowlands received Nahua-speaking migrants from central Mexico, and from the 16th century most of it was ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as the Captaincy General of Guatemala. After independence in 1821 it briefly joined Iturbide's Mexican Empire, then formed a federation, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> held footholds on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/central-america.jpg" alt="Central America" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M.Bitton · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_America_(configurable_orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
